  "id": "ch6-tactics-and-plans",
  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "defaultTags": ["tactics"],
  "intro": "Six positions, each running through a line from an earlier chapter and stopping on the move that matters. Play the line and find the last move yourself. Three of them are about the queen, because the queen out early is the one thing every opponent knows about this opening, and knowing where it goes when it is hit is most of what there is to know.\n\n## What each one is about\n\nThe first is the knight to b5. It hits the queen and eyes c7, and the answer is the retreat that covers c7 rather than the active square that leaves it.\n\nThe second is the check on b5. The pawn that was going to c6 anyway blocks it with tempo, and the bishop has to move again.\n\nThe third is the knight jump. With the queens off and White's king stuck on the open file, a knight comes to b4 and the fork on c2 is in the air.\n\nThe fourth is the pin. The whole structure is built and every piece is placed, and the bishop on g5 is still pinning the knight; the knight to d5 steps out of it, offers a trade that suits Black, and takes the queen out of the bishop's sights.\n\nThe fifth is the break with the c-pawn. The bishop on e4 has been hit and has gone home, the d-pawn has one pawn behind it and nothing else, and the pawn to c5 hits it at the moment the queen is ready to take back.\n\nThe sixth is defence. A knight on g5 and a bishop on c4 are both looking at f7, and one pawn move covers everything.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nAnswering a hit on the queen with the most active square rather than the safest. Blocking a check with a piece when a pawn does it with tempo. Playing a pawn break before the pieces are placed to take back on it. And meeting an attack on f7 by counting attackers, when the pawn to e6 makes the count irrelevant.",
